  {
   "item_id": 852412,
   "item_text": "2",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "984",
   "id": "33558",
   "item_level": "4",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 852412,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 151412,
    "poster_id": 652,
    "post_rendered": "Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > is isosceles <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\">(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c99bbe74cccdc92b249d376f13d9429d41a8b7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=AC$\"  width=\"80\" height=\"13\" >)</span>. From <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"13\" >,</span> we draw a line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"7\" height=\"13\" > parallel to <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >.</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d559f1ae366d6c7ddffa20b504ac135a430839.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" > are on perpendicular bisectors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/5/e956bb95054645df67798a7d96dbb4556f129920.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB,AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/6/da683879f6d25fba80476afe3ce0e7283ff5da5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ\\perp BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"81\" height=\"16\" >.</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/9/1e929dc6c21edf7f11254db489376105e0abc1b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M,N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" > are points on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"7\" height=\"13\" > such that angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/e/63e73e7c0dc52f73f51267f916c97abedd975812.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle APM$\"  width=\"59\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/9230cb2884f67db6d1732bd7036c4060020df683.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AQN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" > are <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/2/1b28eb5c39d0df30d06036f3cf179a5ddeff4d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac\\pi2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"13\" height=\"33\" >.</span> Prove that<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/f/43f8e8bf388cfb9bb161c2d754a7e425004cb65c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{AM}+\\frac1{AN}\\leq\\frac2{AB}\\]\"  width=\"146\" height=\"37\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Triangle $ABC$ is isosceles ($AB=AC$). From $A$, we draw a line $\\ell$ parallel to $BC$. $P,Q$ are on perpendicular bisectors of $AB,AC$ such that $PQ\\perp BC$. $M,N$ are points on $\\ell$ such that angles $\\angle APM$ and $\\angle AQN$ are $\\frac\\pi2$. Prove that\r\n\\[\\frac{1}{AM}+\\frac1{AN}\\leq\\frac2{AB}\\]",
    "username": "Omid Hatami",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_652.png"
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": 852700,
   "item_text": "Click for solution",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post_hidden",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "983",
   "id": "33559",
   "item_level": "3",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 852700,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 151412,
    "poster_id": 18812,
    "post_rendered": "I have a nice and short metric solution, so here it goes:<br>\n<br>\nWe have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/6/126e496b3d2a50c722522ef657d7ddf346087a0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN=\\frac{AQ}{\\cos{QAN}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"128\" height=\"41\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e884f77c86332cf319caa9d94b4d748dfb0a699.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=\\frac{AP}{\\cos{PAM}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"134\" height=\"38\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nBut <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/5/ae58cc7acd014866e81f8c5604f3d0c715097217.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AQ=\\frac{x}{\\cos{QAC}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"124\" height=\"36\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/8/0b835fac770bb8024cd43b02712970862ba6b642.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP=\\frac{x}{\\cos{PAB}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"124\" height=\"33\" >,</span> where <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/1/7516fe187e95be69ed49c6189751eee146bf1915.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{AB}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"65\" height=\"38\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nSo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/2/a22b2b648c1f55a7e9cca2d6e59eda462aedd6ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=\\frac{x}{\\cos{PAM}\\cdot \\cos{PAB}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"217\" height=\"33\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/f/30f7b97afa5d152db2b5672b01a1c4711e313bf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN=\\frac{x}{\\cos{QAN}\\cdot\\cos{QAC}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"211\" height=\"36\" >,</span> so the inequality rewrites as:<br>\n<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/5/3752bfe211a06c0ab18c70c274ce404cda23d014.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{QAC}\\cdot \\cos{QAN}+\\cos{PAM}\\cdot \\cos{PAB}\\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"367\" height=\"16\" ><br>\n<br>\n<span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/6566da1faf15e767d607286818ba84dad76020a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\iff \\cos{B}+\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}+\\cos{BAM}+\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}\\leq 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"512\" height=\"38\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nBut <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/a/aea8833ece563fd27da2dd1edaecace8aa8440d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{B}=180-\\angle{BAM}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"161\" height=\"13\" >,</span> therefore <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/7/257fa9e0ea9ba13a449fd54dbccea0e1400e6b92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{B}=-\\cos{BAM}$\"  width=\"158\" height=\"13\" >,</span> thus the inequality rewrites as <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/4/894abc47c8eb7c731b751a88d2c3691fb945e0dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}\\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"156\" height=\"38\" >,</span> which is true.",
    "post_canonical": "I have a nice and short metric solution, so here it goes:\r\n\r\nWe have that $AN=\\frac{AQ}{\\cos{QAN}}$ and $AM=\\frac{AP}{\\cos{PAM}}$.\r\n\r\nBut $AQ=\\frac{x}{\\cos{QAC}}$ and $AP=\\frac{x}{\\cos{PAB}}$, where $x=\\frac{AB}{2}$.\r\n\r\nSo $AM=\\frac{x}{\\cos{PAM}\\cdot \\cos{PAB}}$ and $AN=\\frac{x}{\\cos{QAN}\\cdot\\cos{QAC}}$, so the inequality rewrites as:\r\n\r\n$\\cos{QAC}\\cdot \\cos{QAN}+\\cos{PAM}\\cdot \\cos{PAB}\\leq 1$\r\n\r\n$\\iff \\cos{B}+\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}+\\cos{BAM}+\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}\\leq 2$.\r\n\r\nBut $\\angle{B}=180-\\angle{BAM}$, therefore $\\cos{B}=-\\cos{BAM}$, thus the inequality rewrites as $\\cos{(QAP-\\frac{A}{2})}\\leq 1$, which is true.",
    "username": "pohoatza",
    "post_number": 3,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_18812.png"
   }
  },
  {
   "item_id": 852428,
   "item_text": "3",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": null,
   "is_watched": null,
   "item_score": "982",
   "id": "33560",
   "item_level": "2",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 852428,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 151420,
    "poster_id": 652,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be a point in a square whose side are mirror. A ray of light comes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and with slope <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\"  width=\"11\" height=\"8\" >.</span> We know that this ray of light never arrives to a vertex. We make an infinite sequence of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/c/a9c68bec0cb7169b21d20277b13cb52aaaf4487e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0,1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" >.</span> After each contact of light ray with a horizontal side, we put <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" >,</span> and after each contact with a vertical side, we put <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" >.</span> For each <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/c/9ccd696a5d0cbab8337ceb8a4cdfa202a54a6eae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" >,</span> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/0/610bb8d6a25ceeb883b9231cdd91040b0edd4b3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" > be set of all blocks of length <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >,</span> in this sequence.<br>\na) Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/0/610bb8d6a25ceeb883b9231cdd91040b0edd4b3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" > does not depend on location of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" >.</span><br>\nb) Prove that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/a/3aa2c396da6dccf4a7962ebbffb0b0d174ff2ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\alpha}{\\pi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"14\" height=\"33\" > is irrational, then <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c1bf3c1c98b793dfcc3faf718b8821c9c1f5a83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|B_{n}|=n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $P$ be a point in a square whose side are mirror. A ray of light comes from $P$ and with slope $\\alpha$. We know that this ray of light never arrives to a vertex. We make an infinite sequence of $0,1$. After each contact of light ray with a horizontal side, we put $0$, and after each contact with a vertical side, we put $1$. For each $n\\geq 1$, let $B_{n}$ be set of all blocks of length $n$, in this sequence.\r\na) Prove that $B_{n}$ does not depend on location of $P$.\r\nb) Prove that if $\\frac{\\alpha}{\\pi}$ is irrational, then $|B_{n}|=n+1$.",
    "username": "Omid Hatami",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_652.png"
   }
  }
